WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:06.871
A statewide radio project needed
836 license-plan reports.

2
00:00:07.271 --> 00:00:11.810
Each one is a PDF, built from the
results of the project's studies.

3
00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:19.799
The project held 185,161 studies
and over eight million results.

4
00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:24.819
One plan took more than ten minutes,
because it scanned the whole results table,

5
00:00:24.819 --> 00:00:26.744
over a billion rows.

6
00:00:27.144 --> 00:00:35.133
Scanning twice per plan, one at a time,
all 836 would have taken about 280 hours.

7
00:00:35.833 --> 00:00:40.784
First I measured where a plan's time
went, and most of it was loading results.

8
00:00:41.184 --> 00:00:46.044
So each plan now finds its studies
first and reads only their results,

9
00:00:46.044 --> 00:00:47.255
not the whole table.

10
00:00:47.555 --> 00:00:50.126
One plan dropped to 20.8 seconds.

11
00:00:50.626 --> 00:00:55.303
Then one pass over the studies finds
the results for every plan at once.

12
00:00:55.603 --> 00:00:57.883
Each contour is prepared once,

13
00:00:57.883 --> 00:01:01.131
instead of once for every
licensee it's checked against.

14
00:01:01.531 --> 00:01:06.850
Instead of adding a proposed eight-gigabyte
index, I narrowed what each plan reads.

15
00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:11.066
Then one background job builds
the plans sixteen at a time.

16
00:01:11.366 --> 00:01:15.591
It shows progress, lists any failure
by plan, and can be cancelled.

17
00:01:16.091 --> 00:01:20.492
All 836 plans finished
in about twelve minutes.

18
00:01:20.892 --> 00:01:22.598
That's under a second per plan.
